# .NET Framework → .NET 8 Migration: BackgroundProcessor

## Key Breaking Changes

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | **Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** |
| `ThreadAbortException` | **Never raised** |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | **Removed** |
| Manual `Thread` + `volatile bool` | Use `CancellationToken` + `Task` |

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass token so blocking calls become cancellable
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token);
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested — replaces ThreadAbortException
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break; // exit loop cleanly; finally still runs
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null)
            return;

        timeout ??= TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2);

        // Signal cancellation (replaces _running = false)
        await _cts.CancelAsync();

        // Wait with a hard timeout (replaces Thread.Abort fallback)
        if (await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(timeout.Value)) != _workerTask)
        {
            // Worker didn't stop in time — log a warning.
            // There is no equivalent to Abort(); you must ensure
            // all blocking calls respect the CancellationToken.
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Critical Pitfalls

**1. Blocking calls that ignore the token are un-cancellable.**
If `GetNextItem()` blocks on a `ManualResetEvent`, a raw socket, or a `BlockingCollection<T>.Take()` without a token, cancellation will never interrupt it. You **must** propagate the token:

```csharp
// ❌ Old — blocks forever, no way to cancel without Abort
var item = queue.Take();

// ✅ New — throws OperationCanceledException on cancel
var item = queue.Take(token);

// ✅ For async channels (preferred in .NET 8)
var item = await channel.Reader.ReadAsync(token);
```

**2. `Thread.ResetAbort()` has no equivalent — and doesn't need one.**
`OperationCanceledException` is a normal exception. It won't auto-rethrow; `finally` blocks run normally. Just remove `ResetAbort()`.

**3. Fire-and-forget `Task.Run` swallows exceptions.**
Observe the task to catch unhandled errors:

```csharp
_workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
// Later, in StopAsync or DisposeAsync:
await _workerTask; // surfaces any unhandled exception
```

**4. `volatile bool` is insufficient for async.**
`CancellationToken` is thread-safe, composable, and works with `await`. Replace all `volatile bool _running` patterns.

**5. No forced termination exists in .NET 8.**
If third-party code truly cannot be cancelled, isolate it in a **separate process** and kill the process — this is the only safe hard-stop mechanism left.
